  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 350 AM EDT Sunday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Continued dry with seasonable temperatures through midweek,\ntrending to above normal late in the week.\n\nThe latest WX analysis continues to show a highly amplified \nupper level pattern, with sfc high pressure centered from \nsouthern New England into the Delmarva. SCT-BKN clouds are \npassing through far southern VA and NE NC, with a mainly clear \nsky elsewhere. Temperatures are currently mostly in the 40s, \nwith pockets of upper 30s over the far north, and 50s at the \nimmediate coast in the SE. Expect readings to fall a few degrees\nthrough sunrise, with patchy frost possible over the far north,\nbut not enough for a Frost Advisory. As the high continues to \nsettle in from the north later today, cloud cover should be less\nthan the past 2 days, but a period of SCT-BKN cumulus are still\nlikely for southern VA and NE NC. High temperatures warm a few \ndegrees with highs in the mid 60s along the coast and upper 60s \nto near 70F across the inland areas. For tonight/early Monday \nmorning will likely see the greatest potential for a more \nwidespread frost spreading east from the piedmont to interior SE\nVA as better decoupling is expected with the sfc high nearly \noverhead. Continued the same thinking as the previous shift to \ngo a few degrees (or several degrees in some areas) colder than \nNBM, with mid 30s forecast for the piedmont (locally lower 30s \npossible) and and mid- upper 30s for interior SE VA. Depending \non trends later today, a Frost Advisory may be considered for \nportions of the area, especially in the NW where temperatures \nare most likely to approach freezing. \n\nAfter the cold start, turning warmer Monday and Tuesday ahead \nof the next (dry) front, then slightly cooler Wednesday and \nstaying dry. Temperatures warm above normal late in the week \nwith high temperatures well into the 70s for most, along with no\nrain chances as moisture is expected to stay offshore off the \nSE US coast. The 25/00z ECMWF ensemble/GEFS chance for \nmeasurable rain (.01\"+) of rain through the end of the month is \nbasically zero aside from some low probs showing up in \nsoutheastern portions of the area later in the week into the \nfollowing weekend, but when switching the threshold to 0.1\" the \nchance is less than 10% area-wide. It should be noted that \ndespite the ongoing drought/precipitation deficit across much of\nthe area, none of our long-term climate sites will rank among \nthe 10 driest Octobers on record (as October has several \noccurrences with monthly rainfall amounts of less than 0.50\"). \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Nuisance flooding expected with today's morning\nto early afternoon high tide cycle.\n\nTidal anomalies will remain elevated today although they continue to \ntrend downward. These elevated anomalies in tandem with the higher \nof the two astronomical tides of the day will push water levels \nclose to minor flooding thresholds this morning into the early \nafternoon for locations adjacent the middle/upper Chesapeake Bay and \nRappahannock River. Coastal Flood Statements have been issued to \naccount for this.",
